## Overview

This system is an internal Certificate Authority that issues and rotates mTLS certificates for millions of workloads and devices with minimal human involvement. The key idea is to stop treating revocation as the primary safety mechanism and instead make certificates *cheap, automated, and short-lived*, so compromise has a small blast radius and rotation is the “normal path,” not an incident procedure.

The design uses an offline root CA, online regional intermediates backed by HSMs, and lightweight Registration Authorities (RAs) close to workloads. Workloads prove identity via an existing identity plane (Kubernetes, device identity, or IAM), generate their own private keys locally, and request certificates through a strict policy engine that binds cert identities to attestable facts (service identity, namespace/project, device serial, etc.).

This stays elegant by isolating the genuinely hard problems—secure key custody + scalable issuance under strict identity/policy—while keeping everything else boring: Postgres for state, object storage for audit artifacts, and simple regional stateless services.

